      <ownedComment xmi:id="_18_0_5_45e01bd_1455720280492_742249_43065" xmi:uuid="3baa91ac-1e95-4df7-8b2c-e74f262ae2f5" xmi:type="uml:Comment">
        <annotatedElement xmi:idref="_18_4_1_e120334_1499417193194_729680_23768"/>
        <body>A ProductClass is a type of ProductConcept that represents a set of similar products to be offered to the market. ProductClass objects that are related to each other by a ProductClassRelationship do not inherit any characteristics from each other. &lt;note&gt; The definition of product classes is driven by market and customer requirements and forecasting. A ProductClass often corresponds to the highest-level item(s) manufactured by an organization for customers. &lt;/note&gt;&lt;example&gt; In an organization which manufactures cars and engines for cars, the car models will be represented by ProductClass objects. &lt;/example&gt;&lt;note&gt; A ProductClass enables to represent customer-oriented identification of products that are to be delivered to customers, while a Part enables to identify and to track the history of items that are designed and manufactured, as a tangible solution, or component of the solution, for a ProductClass. &lt;/note&gt;&lt;note&gt; A ProductClass may be characterized by a set of features or specifications identified by the customers or derived from customers' needs. &lt;/note&gt;&lt;note&gt; Depending on the kind of industry and products, a ProductClass might be offered to the customers in one single or in many different configurations. &lt;/note&gt;&lt;example&gt; The Figure &lt;caption&gt;Example structure from the automotive domain of ProductClass objects in a hierarchy&lt;/caption&gt; depicts a detail of an example of a structure that is built up by ProductClass and ProductClassRelationship objects. &lt;/example&gt;&lt;figure&gt;&lt;name&gt;example_product_class_hierarchy.gif&lt;/name&gt;Example structure from the automotive domain of ProductClass objects in a hierarchy&lt;/figure&gt;</body>
      </ownedComment>
      <name>ProductClass</name>
      <generalization xmi:id="_18_4_1_e120334_1499417193942_122903_28381" xmi:uuid="069540d1-1a0f-483f-bc98-6fec58eb3c07" xmi:type="uml:Generalization">
